Q: Unsure how to go about replicating React Component lifecycle methods through useEffect() where componentDidMount calls a function within a function
A: <p>I would like to suggest the following:</p>
<ul>
<li>If you are using functional components, work with the 'useState' hook to manage the state. You can later reference the destructured variables and methods freely as long as they are within the scope of the component, without using 'this'.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>const [scripts, setScripts] = useState();

// Inside render:
&lt;h1&gt; {scripts.length} &lt;/h1&gt;
// ...
&lt;button onClick={() =&gt; setScripts([])}&gt; Erase Scripts &lt;/button&gt;
</code></pre>
<ul>
<li>Same concept with methods you create:</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>const handleScriptButtonClick = () =&gt; {};

// Inside render:
&lt;button onClick={handleScriptButtonClick}&gt; Script &lt;/button&gt;
</code></pre>
<ul>
<li><p>Try to avoid using indices as keys in arrays if you can. Ideally find some other unique identifier.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Try to avoid using the state to store a big list of elements or components. It's preferable to store the data associated with the buttons you want to create and then build them inside the render method. Something like this:</p>
</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>render() {
// ...
&lt;section&gt;
      { scripts.map((script, i) =&gt; 
        &lt;button key={script.id} onClick={() =&gt; handleScriptButtonClick(script)}&gt;
          Script {i}
        &lt;/button&gt;
      )}
 &lt;/section&gt;
}
</code></pre>

Q: React useEffect() only run on first render with dependencies
A: <p>The way I handle this now is to put the appropriate dependencies in the list of dependencies.</p>
<p>Because I want the effect to run only one time, and because the effect only relies on some data when the component first mounts, it's perfectly fine to omit those dependencies. For example, the <code>groups</code> prop may change later, but this effect doesn't need to run again.</p>
<p>But, as a habit I don't omit the recommended dependencies and I always list them. If I were to intentionally omit something, I would add an eslint ignore statement... it's whatever convention you want to follow as long as you understand what is happening when that data changes and the effect does / does not run.</p>
<p>However the code I proposed, shown below, isn't the best solution if you do want to list the dependencies as it causes an extra render when <code>didLoad</code> changes.</p>
<pre><code> const [didLoad, setDidLoad] = useState&lt;boolean&gt;(false);

  useEffect(() =&gt; {
    if (!didLoad) {
      container.current = new VisTimeline(container.current, items, groups, options);
      setDidLoad(true);
    }
  }, [didLoad, groups, items, options]);
</code></pre>
<p>Instead of using state to track that the effect ran, I will use a ref (which doesn't need to be a dependency).</p>
<pre><code> const timelineLoaded = useRef&lt;boolean&gt;(false);

  useEffect(() =&gt; {
    if (!timelineLoaded.current) {
      container.current = new VisTimeline(container.current, items, groups, options);
      timelineLoaded.current = true;
    }
  }, [groups, items, options]);
</code></pre>

